% Exactly four overview rows, distinct from the expansive study's lane senses.
\zlabel{triptych:concise:overview:start}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{0.18\linewidth}X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Sense} & \textbf{Synthesis}\\\midrule
\zlabel{triptych:concise:sense:literal}Literal & Isaiah calls for return; the psalm praises the Lord's mercy and nearness; Paul weighs being with Christ against fruitful service; the landowner keeps his promise while welcoming late workers. The prayers ask for love to become obedience and sacramental reception to become conduct.\\
\zlabel{triptych:concise:sense:allegorical}Allegorical & Christ cultivates hearts and gathers his people. Augustine's fruitful gift, Chrysostom's vineyard of commandments, and Gregory's Church through the ages illuminate different dimensions of the one caller. Communion accents either enabled obedience or belonging to the self-giving Shepherd.\\
\zlabel{triptych:concise:sense:moral}Moral & Receive mercy without despair or deliberate delay. Seek God himself, attend to his word, and make another's good a reason to work. Early service creates responsibility for others, never ownership of their admission or a claim that their portion must be smaller.\\
\zlabel{triptych:concise:sense:anagogical}Anagogical & The common hope is life with Christ, shared without rivalry. Augustine's endless denarius preserves different glory; Chrysostom excludes envy from heaven; Gregory sees earlier and later generations gathered into the kingdom opened by Christ.\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\zlabel{triptych:concise:overview:end}
\clearpage
